+### GPU Texture Upload and Composition
+#### definition
+Draws the voxel map to a GPU texture, optionally compositing with rasterized scene objects [S2, unofficial].
+#### step
+Dispatch compute work groups based on the resolution of `outputTexture` to render the voxel map into it [S2, unofficial].
+#### parameter
+`outputTexture`: `GLuint` handle to the target texture. Must have internal format `GL_RGBA32F`. Its resolution determines the number of compute work groups dispatched [S2, unofficial].
+`rasterColorTexture`: `int` handle to the color buffer for rasterized objects. Use `-1` to disable composition [S2, unofficial].
+`rasterDepthTexture`: `int` handle to the depth buffer for rasterized objects. Use `-1` to disable composition [S2, unofficial].
+#### pitfall
+`outputTexture` must be `GL_RGBA32F`; using a different format will cause undefined behavior or rendering failure [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concepts originate from CUDA-accelerated volume rendering engines, specifically the cuVRender project, which implements scene management, volume description, and device-side data structures for efficient raycasting [S1, unofficial]. The GPU Data Layout pattern is common in VTK-based volume rendering mappers, while ClearView Unified Buffer Construction is specific to the ClearView renderer's hexahedral mesh processing pipeline [S1, unofficial].
+The concepts in this family originate from CUDA-accelerated volume rendering engines and hybrid rendering pipelines. The "GPU Data Layout" and "Device Volume Snapshot" structures are derived from the `cuVRender` engine, which uses a `Scene` class to manage camera, volume, transfer function, and light state, committing snapshots to device memory for kernel execution [S1, unofficial]. The "ClearView Unified Buffer Construction" is specific to the ClearView renderer, which optimizes hexahedral mesh rendering by precomputing topology into 2D textures [S1, unofficial]. The "GPU Texture Upload and Composition" technique is associated with the DoonEngine, which integrates voxel maps with rasterized scenes using compute shaders [S2, unofficial]. These methods are used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and real-time volume rendering applications where performance and memory efficiency are critical.
 
